**SUMMARY OF THE INVENTION**

The present disclosure addresses the technical limitations inherent in conventional pharmaceutical packaging lines—specifically, spatial inefficiencies, print registration errors caused by vibration along extended transfer paths, and ink contamination risks associated with side-mounted or off-line printing units. 

To overcome these deficiencies, the present invention provides a compact, high-precision drug packaging machine [100] featuring an integrated overhead inkjet printing assembly [110] directly incorporated into a continuous, stabilized conveyance path [104].

In accordance with one aspect of the present disclosure, a drug packaging machine [100] includes a base frame [102] supporting a continuous conveyance assembly [104]. The conveyance assembly [104] defines a linear conveyance path extending longitudinally along an $X$-axis from an upstream loading zone [104a] to a downstream discharge zone [104b]. The conveyance assembly [104] is configured to advance a packaging substrate web [106] continuously or semi-continuously along a horizontal planar surface defined by the $X$-axis and a transverse $Y$-axis.

An overhead printing assembly [110] is rigidly mounted to a bridge gantry [114] fixedly secured to the base frame [102]. The bridge gantry [114] spans transversely across the $Y$-axis of the conveyance assembly [104] and extends vertically upward along a $Z$-axis, orthogonal to the $X\text{-}Y$ plane. The overhead printing assembly [110] comprises at least one piezo-electric inkjet printhead array [112] positioned vertically above ($+Z$ direction) the conveyance path. 

Specifically, the face of the inkjet printhead array [112] is maintained at a fixed, predetermined standoff gap ($d_1$) along the $Z$-axis relative to the upper surface of the packaging substrate web [106]. In a preferred embodiment, this standoff gap ($d_1$) is maintained between $0.5\text{ mm}$ and $2.0\text{ mm}$ to prevent mechanical interference with raised drug-containing pockets formed in the web [106] while minimizing droplet flight deviation.

Relative to the spatial sequence along the longitudinal conveyance axis ($X$-axis), the packaging machine [100] comprises the following components in strict spatial and operational order from upstream ($-X$) to downstream ($+X$):

1. **A web-forming and feeding station [103]**, positioned at the far upstream terminus ($-X$) of the conveyance path, configured to supply or form pockets within the packaging substrate web [106];
2. **A drug dosing station [108]**, positioned downstream ($+X$) of the web-forming station [103] and upstream ($-X$) of the overhead printing assembly [110], configured to deposit solid or liquid dosage units into the web [106];
3. **An optical web-registration sensor [120]**, positioned immediately upstream ($-X$) of the overhead printing assembly [110] and vertically adjacent to the conveyance plane, configured to detect registration marks on the web [106];
4. **The overhead printing assembly [110]**, suspended above ($+Z$) the conveyance path, operatively coupled to the optical web-registration sensor [120] to dynamically apply variable text, bar codes, or serialization data directly to the top surface of the web [106] in real-time alignment with individual package pockets;
5. **A UV-LED curing module [116]**, positioned downstream ($+X$) of the inkjet printhead array [112] at a distance $d_2$ along the $X$-axis, suspended vertically above ($+Z$) the web [106] to instantly cure the printed ink before contact with downstream mechanical elements;
6. **A vision inspection system [122]**, mounted downstream ($+X$) of the curing module [116] and oriented facing downward ($-Z$ direction) toward the web [106], configured to verify print quality and data legibility; and
7. **A lamination and dicing assembly [118]**, positioned at the downstream terminus ($+X$) of the conveyance path, configured to seal a secondary film to the web [106] and singulate the web into discrete, individual drug packages [124].

By arranging the inkjet printing assembly [110] directly overhead ($+Z$) along the unified linear conveyance path ($X$-axis) between the dosing station [108] and the downstream lamination assembly [118], the present invention eliminates the need for intermediate transfer belts or off-line printing modules. This spatial arrangement reduces the footprint of the overall machine [100], eliminates mechanical web-drift between printing and sealing, and ensures precise, sub-millimeter print registration across high-speed packaging cycles.

A detailed description of the preferred embodiments, accompanied by illustrative drawings, follows to further elucidate the structural and functional relationships of the present invention.
